---
description: Workit coordination boundaries and truthful Cursor capabilities
alwaysApply: true
---

Workit is an optional coordinator, not a permission system. Ordinary questions,
investigation, and reversible edits need no Workit task, assessment,
reviewer, or close call; follow Cursor's native permissions. When a step has one
right answer, run the `workit` CLI verb for it (`workit check`, `workit git …`,
`workit pr …`, `workit ledger …`). Use one compact
Workit record when handoff, dependent steps, concurrent actors, or important
decisions make durable continuity useful. Do not duplicate a shared task per
host or merge distinct intents because their titles look similar.

Workit policy may narrow an action, but it never grants host access or overrides
a host deny. Check the actual target checkout, configured account, branch and
commit conventions, protected refs, and expected remote state before a delivery
verb; merge and release need a workspace grant (`workit grant show`). Report unsupported shell coverage honestly.

Cursor hooks provide evidence only where their documented input and blocking
behavior support it. AskQuestion answers are policy-only; `sessionStart` and
`preCompact` cannot block; `subagentStop` has no stable child identity; arbitrary
shell writes and Tab edits are unavailable. Never invent a caller
identity or cross-process caller channel. For native subagents in tracked
Workit work, begin the task payload with exactly one assigned role marker:
`[workit-role: implementer]`, `[workit-role: reviewer]`, or
`[workit-role: investigator]`.

Cursor cannot attribute subagent writes, so Workit never enforces a
subagent's write scope here. Fanout writers (workit-fanout) are agent-guided:
each runs in its own git worktree with a fixed brief, and its file scope is
checked at fan-in with `git diff --name-only <base>...<branch>`, not enforced
while it works. Verifiers and reviewers stay read-only and record
`workit ledger verdict` under a session the lead assigns
(`WORKIT_SESSION_ID=<lead>-v<n>` in the brief), never the author's.
